  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: a4da58d2dc2409c459ef20449bf92a90, type: 3}
  m_Name: LiveLoadLesson
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  lessonId: live-load
  lessonTitle: Live Load
  lessonImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 2db204386ad37a845b49016eb72888b4, type: 3}
  lessonDescription: "Imagine a bridge standing perfectly still. The permanent weight
    of the steel, concrete, cables, and asphalt makes up what engineers call the
    \"Dead Load.\" But bridges aren't built to just stand empty\u2014they are built
    to be used!\n\nThat is where the Live Load comes in.\n\nA Live Load is any temporary,
    moving, or changing weight that is placed on a structure. For a bridge, live
    loads include cars, heavy cargo trucks, trains, and even crowds of people walking
    across.\n\nWhy are Live Loads an engineering challenge?\nUnlike the bridge's
    own weight, which never changes, live loads are constantly moving. When a heavy
    delivery vehicle drives across your bridge, it shifts its weight from one section
    of the road to the next. This means different beams, joints, and supports will
    experience intense stress at different times. A beam that is perfectly fine when
    the truck is at the start of the bridge might suddenly snap when the truck reaches
    the middle!\n\nThe Engineer's Job:\nWhen designing your bridge, you cannot just
    make it strong enough to hold itself up. You must build a structure capable of
    supporting the heaviest possible Live Load, no matter where that load is currently
    positioned on the bridge.\n\n"
